    "abstract": "Food insecurity remains a critical and persistent development challenge in Africa, exacerbated by accelerating climate change, recurrent conflicts, and weak institutional capacity. While these factors have been widely studied in isolation, limited empirical evidence exists on their joint and interactive effects on food security (FS) across the continent. This study examines the individual, combined, and moderating roles of climate change, institutional quality (INSQ), and conflict in shaping food security outcomes in 27 African countries over the period 2012\u20132021. Using panel data from multiple international sources, the study employs fixed effects and system generalized method of moments (GMM) estimators to address unobserved heterogeneity and endogeneity concerns. The results show that rising temperatures and drought significantly undermine food security, while conflict independently reduces food security. Importantly, the interaction between conflict and climate stressors\u2014particularly temperature and drought\u2014amplifies food insecurity outcomes. In contrast, higher institutional quality and increased agricultural investment significantly improve food security and mitigate the adverse effects of both climate change and conflict. These findings underscore the central role of effective governance in building resilient food systems. The study highlights the need for integrated policy approaches that simultaneously strengthen institutional capacity, promote climate-smart agricultural investments, and incorporate conflict-sensitive strategies to achieve sustainable food security in Africa.",
    "field": "Climate-conflict-food security nexus",
    "problem": "Existing research has widely studied climate change, conflict, and institutional quality in isolation, but limited empirical evidence exists on their joint and interactive effects on food security in Africa within a unified framework.",
    "method": "This study employs fixed effects and system generalized method of moments (GMM) estimators on panel data from 27 African countries to examine the individual, combined, and interactive effects of climate change, institutional quality, and conflict on food security outcomes between 2012 and 2021.",
    "task": "The task is to examine the individual, combined, and moderating impacts of climate change, institutional quality, and conflict on food security outcomes across 27 African countries from 2012\u20132021.",
