            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The negative effects of rising temperatures and drought on food security observed in this study are consistent with a substantial body of literature documenting climate-induced agricultural vulnerability in Africa. Studies such as Hatfield et al. (2011), Pickson and Boateng (2022), and Omokpariola et al. (2025) similarly find that increasing temperatures reduce crop yields and exacerbate food shortages, particularly in rain-fed agricultural systems that dominate Sub-Saharan Africa. The results reinforce these findings by demonstrating that temperature increases and drought shocks significantly reduce food security outcomes across a broad panel of African countries.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Studies such as Pickson and Boateng (2022) and Onyeneke et al. (2017) support the assertion that climate change has a substantial effect on the food security status of African countries. The results support existing studies and find that rising temperatures and drought negatively impact food security in Africa. Increasing temperature levels and drought undermine food security through reduced crop yields and water unavailability, which drives up food prices (Hatfield et al., 2011). This is especially true in sub-Saharan Africa, where agriculture is mainly rainfall-dependent due to the unavailability of irrigation infrastructure. Reduced crop yield ultimately affect total food production, which causes food price hikes due to widespread food shortages.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "sub-Saharan Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Studies such as Pickson and Boateng (2022) and Onyeneke et al. (2017) support the assertion that climate change has a substantial effect on the food security status of African countries. The results support existing studies and find that rising temperatures and drought negatively impact food security in Africa. Increasing temperature levels and drought undermine food security through reduced crop yields and water unavailability, which drives up food prices (Hatfield et al., 2011). This is especially true in sub-Saharan Africa, where agriculture is mainly rainfall-dependent due to the unavailability of irrigation infrastructure. Reduced crop yield ultimately affect total food production, which causes food price hikes due to widespread food shortages.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Africa",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Given the significant negative effects of rising temperatures and drought on food security, African governments should prioritize climate-resilient agricultural investments. These include expanding access to drought-tolerant and heat-resistant crop varieties, scaling up small-scale irrigation schemes, and promoting climate-smart agronomic practices such as conservation agriculture and agroforestry. These interventions directly mitigate the production shocks associated with climate stress by stabilizing yields under adverse weather conditions. Importantly, policies should focus on smallholder farmers, who dominate African agriculture and are disproportionately exposed to climate risks due to reliance on rain-fed systems. Regional coordination through bodies such as ECOWAS, COMESA, and the African Union could facilitate knowledge sharing and joint climate adaptation initiatives. Also, given the cross-border nature of both climate and conflict",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
